1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,840
I'm here at Harvard in the office of Steven Pinker.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,000
And he's written nine books and devoted his life

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,640
to studying language and cognition and writing.

4
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:09,900
And so what we did in this interview

5
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:11,940
is we started off with the really practical stuff.

6
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:14,460
We started off with his rules for writing.

7
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:16,040
And what makes him unique is that he's

8
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:19,040
been thinking about AI since the 1980s.

9
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:22,260
So if you're interested in doing great nonfiction writing

10
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:23,880
in the age of LLMs,

11
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:25,500
well, this interview is for you.

12
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:30,180
I want to talk about The Curse of Knowledge.

13
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:31,950
And I want to talk about

14
00:00:31,950 --> 00:00:33,720
this cartoon from your book, which

15
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:37,080
says, good start needs more gibberish.

16
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:37,920
Yes.

17
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:39,780
When I posed the question, why

18
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:41,640
is there so much bad writing?

19
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:44,519
Why is there so much academies and bureaucraties

20
00:00:44,519 --> 00:00:46,500
and corporaties?

21
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,440
People's favorite answer is captured by that cartoon,

22
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:52,980
namely that bad writing is a deliberate choice.

23
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:55,100
It's in various versions.

24
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:59,480
It's academics with nothing to say dress up banal ideas

25
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:02,720
with gobbledygook to show how sophisticated they are.

26
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:04,700
Or pasty-faced nerds get revenge

27
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:06,680
on the girls who turned them

28
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:08,660
down for dates in high school.

29
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:13,520
People want to erect a kind of cult

30
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:16,040
that no one else, outsiders, can't penetrate because they

31
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:18,540
haven't learned the jargon.

32
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:22,760
I don't think that's the best explanation for bad writing.

33
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:23,760
Partly, it's personal.

34
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:25,080
I just know enough people.

35
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:28,020
I know enough people who have plenty to say.

36
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:29,520
They're brilliant people.

37
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:32,700
They have no desire to obfuscate.

38
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:34,560
They're just incompetent.

39
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:37,740
They just don't know how to express themselves clearly.

40
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:40,080
There's something called Hanlon's razor.

41
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:43,620
Never attribute to malice that which can adequately

42
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:44,940
be explained by stupidity.

43
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:45,840
Yeah.

44
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:49,680
And these are not stupid people I'm talking about,

45
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:52,070
but it's a kind of stupidity in

46
00:01:52,070 --> 00:01:54,460
not knowing where your audience is coming from.

47
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:57,329
And I illustrate it with an

48
00:01:57,329 --> 00:02:00,199
anecdote of a conference in technology,

49
00:02:00,199 --> 00:02:04,699
entertainment, and design, better known by its acronym, TED,

50
00:02:04,699 --> 00:02:08,969
where a brilliant molecular biologist had

51
00:02:08,969 --> 00:02:13,240
been invited to present his latest findings.

52
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:16,010
He launched into what was obvious to

53
00:02:16,010 --> 00:02:18,780
me as the exact kind of talk

54
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:22,940
that he would give to his peers in molecular biology.

55
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:24,120
And that,

56
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:27,530
within about four seconds, he had lost everyone

57
00:02:27,530 --> 00:02:30,600
because he just spoke in jargon without even

58
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:32,360
introducing what problem he was

59
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:34,120
solving, why it was significant.

60
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:37,300
It was launched right into the experiments.

61
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:39,820
There was a room of several hundred

62
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:42,340
people from many walks of life, from entertainment,

63
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:43,640
from design.

64
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:45,710
And it was obvious to everyone in the

65
00:02:45,710 --> 00:02:47,780
room that no one was understanding a word.

66
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:50,180
Obvious to everyone except the

67
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:52,580
distinguished biologist, who was just clueless.

68
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:54,240
Now, this is not a stupid man.

69
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:57,420
He was very stupid when it came to

70
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:00,380
communication, namely, not everyone knows what you know.

71
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:04,380
Now, the curse of knowledge, a term from economics,

72
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:08,380
is the difficulty that we all have in knowing

73
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:12,100
what it's like not to know something that we know.

74
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:15,730
That is to subtract something from your brain,

75
00:03:15,730 --> 00:03:19,360
put yourself in the shoes of your audience,

76
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:22,400
whether it be in public speaking or in writing.

77
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:23,620
Figure out where they're coming from.

78
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:23,970
What do they know? What don't they know?

79
00:03:23,970 --> 00:03:24,320
And you know what? What I'm talking about?

80
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:25,700
don't they know?

81
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:29,000
I think that's the main cause of bad writing.

82
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,260
You get abbreviations and acronyms

83
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:34,100
that no one has any way of knowing.

84
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:36,240
You have jargon that is known

85
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:38,380
only to a tiny little clique.

86
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:40,920
You have abstractions.

87
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:47,380
The level of the stimulus was proportional to the intensity

88
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:48,859
of the reaction.

89
00:03:48,859 --> 00:03:51,489
And what it really means is

90
00:03:51,489 --> 00:03:54,120
that kids look longer at a bunny

91
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:57,000
than a truck.

92
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,280
So that is, it's so familiar to you

93
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:01,280
that you don't think it's

94
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:03,280
worth explaining to people concretely

95
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:04,920
what they're supposed to be seeing.

96
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:06,660
So all of these are manifestations

97
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:07,780
of the curse of knowledge.

98
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:10,740
Goes by other names, egocentrism, absence

99
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:12,360
of a theory of mind.

100
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:13,890
That is a theory of what's

101
00:04:13,890 --> 00:04:15,420
going on in other people's minds.

102
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:18,970
And if I had to identify

103
00:04:18,970 --> 00:04:22,520
the single biggest flaw in writing

104
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:23,700
and communication, it would be that.

105
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:24,100
That's it.

106
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:24,600
That's it.

107
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:25,980
So when you're writing your books,

108
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:27,400
I know you go up to Cape

109
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:28,820
Cod and you write for as long

110
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:30,180
as you possibly can.

111
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:32,260
Now, I would assume that one way to get around

112
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:33,480
the curse of knowledge is just to talk

113
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:34,720
to the kinds of people

114
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:35,960
who would be reading your book.

115
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:36,800
Yeah, you show it to people.

116
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:37,460
So what do you do?

117
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:37,900
What do you do?

118
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:39,920
Because you write really intensely.

119
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:42,000
So when you're writing, how do you get around that?

120
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:43,260
Well, I do something that

121
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:44,520
I know is not good enough,

122
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:46,210
but I do it as

123
00:04:46,210 --> 00:04:47,900
best I can, which is I

124
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:50,460
try to imagine what it's like for someone

125
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:51,500
not to know what I know.

126
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:53,680
That is, I try to cultivate my sense of empathy.

127
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:55,300
But the problem with the curse of knowledge

128
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:57,440
is you don't know when you're subject to the curse

129
00:04:57,440 --> 00:05:02,320
of knowledge, because something that seems so obvious to you

130
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:04,900
that you don't even question whether other people know,

131
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:06,680
it turns out not to be obvious.

132
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:08,520
So anyway, I try.

133
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:09,590
But at the end of the

134
00:05:09,590 --> 00:05:10,660
day, I show it to people.

135
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:12,220
When my mother was alive, I would always

136
00:05:12,220 --> 00:05:15,040
show her a draft of my book.

137
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:19,000
Not for the reason that most academics cite,

138
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,680
namely referring to my mother as the most

139
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:28,420
epitome of a unsophisticated, not very well-read,

140
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:29,800
not very bright person.

141
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:34,060
My mother was extremely intelligent, extremely well-read,

142
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:35,520
and very sophisticated.

143
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:37,560
But she wasn't a cognitive psychologist.

144
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:38,940
She wasn't a psycholinguist.

145
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:40,900
She didn't know what I knew.

146
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:43,480
And when I write, I don't

147
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:46,060
write for just a random sample

148
00:05:46,060 --> 00:05:46,800
of the population.

149
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:49,000
They don't buy my books.

150
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,520
I write for people who are intellectually curious, who

151
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:53,660
have some degree of education.

152
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:58,000
However, not for peers in my field.

153
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
And so my mother being an example.

154
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,920
But also, of course, when you publish

155
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:03,920
for a commercial publisher, you have an editor.

156
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:06,680
And the editor is typically very smart,

157
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:08,860
but again, not in your field.

158
00:06:08,860 --> 00:06:11,080
And I show it to people in different fields

159
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:12,580
who are academics.

160
00:06:12,580 --> 00:06:18,240
But it's surprising how insular even academics are when

161
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:20,120
it comes to other academics.

162
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:23,300
Sometimes there'll be people here in this building,

163
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:26,240
in my own department, sometimes my own subdivision

164
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:28,460
within my own department, like students.

165
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:30,780
And they'll give me their thesis proposal.

166
00:06:30,780 --> 00:06:31,920
And I just don't know what they're talking about.

167
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:33,820
Sometimes in my own field, because they've

168
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:36,180
been immersed in like five

169
00:06:36,180 --> 00:06:38,540
or six people in their lab.

170
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:41,060
Their supervisor and the other grad students,

171
00:06:41,060 --> 00:06:43,180
a couple of postdocs, and a research assistant.

172
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:46,060
And they've all been consuming the same jargon,

173
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:48,080
that as soon as they step outside

174
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:51,920
that tiny little circle, they're unintelligible.

175
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:53,240
So even showing it to.

176
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:55,980
If I'm showing it to a friend who's

177
00:06:55,980 --> 00:06:58,880
an economist, or a historian, or a political scientist,

178
00:06:58,880 --> 00:07:01,040
or an evolutionary biologist, not

179
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:03,140
being a cognitive psychologist, they'll say,

180
00:07:03,140 --> 00:07:04,260
I'm sorry, but I just

181
00:07:04,260 --> 00:07:05,380
don't know what you're talking about.

182
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:06,020
Yeah.

183
00:07:06,020 --> 00:07:07,250
In terms of your writing,

184
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:08,480
you've done so much work looking

185
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:10,100
at vision and how the brain works.

186
00:07:10,100 --> 00:07:11,980
And it seems like a lot of your writing advice

187
00:07:11,980 --> 00:07:12,940
is informed by that.

188
00:07:12,940 --> 00:07:14,020
Like, I guess if I

189
00:07:14,020 --> 00:07:15,100
were to try to summarize it,

190
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:17,090
it's like a lot of

191
00:07:17,090 --> 00:07:19,080
our brain and basically the way

192
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:20,940
that we move through the

193
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:22,800
world is indexed heavily on vision.

194
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:27,000
And so writing well means being concrete and helping people

195
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,740
see what it is they're trying to write.

196
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:30,040
Is that a good way of describing it?

197
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:30,540
Yes.

198
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:34,500
And that would be probably the second bit of advice

199
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:35,120
on the list.

200
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:35,620
OK.

201
00:07:35,620 --> 00:07:37,120
The first one being, find some way

202
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:38,740
of getting into your reader's heads.

203
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:40,180
But don't just depend on

204
00:07:40,180 --> 00:07:41,620
your ability to get their heads.

205
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:43,010
Actually, get a flesh and

206
00:07:43,010 --> 00:07:44,400
blood person to actually read it

207
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:45,900
and see if it makes any sense to them.

208
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:46,940
So that'd be number one.

209
00:07:46,940 --> 00:07:49,820
Number two is, I study language.

210
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:52,000
And as a writer, I live in language.

211
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:52,500
Yeah.

212
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:55,300
But language is kind of overrated in the sense

213
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:57,550
that what understanding consists of

214
00:07:57,550 --> 00:07:59,800
is not a bunch of words.

215
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:01,800
It's not blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

216
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:03,660
But language is a means to an end

217
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:07,900
of getting people to appreciate the ideas you're

218
00:08:07,900 --> 00:08:09,680
trying to convey, which are

219
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:11,460
not just a string of verbiage.

220
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:16,300
And those ideas very often are visual and motoric,

221
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:20,740
that is bodily, emotional, auditory.

222
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:22,000
But they're sensory.

223
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:22,500
Hm.

224
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:24,800
Or they're even conceptual.

225
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:28,300
But they aren't just a bunch of vowels and consonants.

226
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:29,180
Right.

227
00:08:29,180 --> 00:08:33,679
And so constantly allowing your reader

228
00:08:33,679 --> 00:08:36,080
to be able to form a

229
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:38,480
mental image based on what you're writing

230
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:41,520
is the next key to good writing.

231
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:44,680
That is, don't talk about a stimulus

232
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:47,400
if you mean a bunny rabbit.

233
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:48,360
Right.

234
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:50,500
Don't talk about a level

235
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:52,640
or a perspective or a framework.

236
00:08:52,800 --> 00:08:55,140
Or a paradigm or a concept.

237
00:08:55,140 --> 00:08:56,970
All of which mean a lot

238
00:08:56,970 --> 00:08:58,800
to you in your day-to-day work.

239
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:01,550
But no one can form an image

240
00:09:01,550 --> 00:09:04,300
of a paradigm in their mind's eye.

241
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:05,180
So how do you do that?

242
00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:07,370
Like, one thing that you've said

243
00:09:07,370 --> 00:09:09,560
is use for example all the time.

244
00:09:09,560 --> 00:09:12,420
What else can we do?

245
00:09:12,420 --> 00:09:17,380
So often visual metaphors are helpful.

246
00:09:17,380 --> 00:09:20,090
One of the reasons that often the prose

247
00:09:20,090 --> 00:09:22,800
of other eras strikes us as so much,

248
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:24,800
much more vivid is that.

249
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:25,800
I was going to say lush.

250
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:26,520
Lush.

251
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:27,479
Yes.

252
00:09:27,479 --> 00:09:31,009
Partly because they had the

253
00:09:31,009 --> 00:09:34,540
advantage of writing before there were

254
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:37,740
several hundred years of academia and intellectuals

255
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:40,740
inventing terms and abstractions.

256
00:09:40,740 --> 00:09:45,120
And they had to appeal to images that were

257
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:46,680
part of people's common knowledge.

258
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:49,859
So instead of saying something like aggression

259
00:09:49,859 --> 00:09:52,680
or antisocial behavior, they might say,

260
00:09:52,680 --> 00:09:56,239
the spirit of the hawk kneaded into our flesh.

261
00:09:56,239 --> 00:09:57,800
Yes.

262
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:00,760
We wouldn't write like that because we can say aggression

263
00:10:00,760 --> 00:10:02,520
or antisocial behavior.

264
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:04,359
But that's jargon that a

265
00:10:04,359 --> 00:10:06,199
lot of people are familiar with.

266
00:10:06,199 --> 00:10:07,439
But they weren't always with us.

267
00:10:07,439 --> 00:10:09,089
And before that, there had to be

268
00:10:09,089 --> 00:10:10,739
some way of referring to it in terms

269
00:10:10,739 --> 00:10:12,359
of an image that everyone shared.

270
00:10:12,359 --> 00:10:13,979
And I think that's why

271
00:10:13,979 --> 00:10:15,599
often the writing of previous centuries

272
00:10:15,599 --> 00:10:18,229
just strikes us as so much more

273
00:10:18,229 --> 00:10:20,859
gripping, so much more evocative and powerful

274
00:10:20,859 --> 00:10:22,479
that they had to appeal to.

275
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:23,680
And that's why we're so proud

276
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:24,880
of the fact that we have

277
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:26,920
this kind of visual metaphors.

278
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:27,420
Yeah.

279
00:10:27,420 --> 00:10:28,920
I've never thought about that before.

280
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:30,140
Because a lot of the Bibles

281
00:10:30,140 --> 00:10:31,360
like that, I think of like,

282
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:32,540
I don't know why the

283
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:33,720
owl of Minerva came to mind.

284
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:36,520
There's just all of this symbolism in animals.

285
00:10:36,520 --> 00:10:38,800
It always is striking.

286
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:40,300
And so what you're saying

287
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:41,800
is a lot of the concepts

288
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:44,260
that academics and people brought in, obviously,

289
00:10:44,260 --> 00:10:46,960
they're kind of a more efficient way to communicate.

290
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:49,600
But they do lack that kind of visual quality

291
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:50,900
that makes writing vivid.

292
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:51,760
That's right.

293
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:54,760
And so you can't be a biologist or a biologist

294
00:10:54,760 --> 00:10:56,319
and do all of that stuff.

295
00:10:56,319 --> 00:10:58,480
But you can talk about things like the universe,

296
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:02,620
or doing the work within the profession.

297
00:11:02,620 --> 00:11:05,300
You have to be able, if you're a biologist,

298
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:07,160
you have to be able

299
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:09,020
to talk about things like ecosystems,

300
00:11:09,020 --> 00:11:14,620
and species, and systems, and reagents, and potentiation.

301
00:11:14,620 --> 00:11:16,610
All these concepts that you don't want

302
00:11:16,610 --> 00:11:18,600
to have to go back to basics

303
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:21,200
and talk about, well, there's a certain amount of chemicals.

304
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:24,080
And so you have more and more abstract terms

305
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:26,960
that you could refer to, enormous bodies of knowledge,

306
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:28,580
just with two syllables.

307
00:11:28,580 --> 00:11:29,980
That's very good.

308
00:11:29,980 --> 00:11:31,740
The problem is that then now

309
00:11:31,740 --> 00:11:33,500
when it's time to convey them

310
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:37,380
to someone who isn't at the pinnacle of specialization

311
00:11:37,380 --> 00:11:40,160
in your field, because of the curse of knowledge,

312
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:43,440
you're apt to forget that these abstractions, which

313
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:45,760
are basic to you, or just

314
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:48,080
don't even need to be defined,

315
00:11:48,080 --> 00:11:49,320
aren't basic to anyone else.

316
00:11:49,320 --> 00:11:49,820
Hm.

317
00:11:49,820 --> 00:11:51,200
What do we need to know about writing?

318
00:11:51,200 --> 00:11:52,490
And I think that's where you

319
00:11:52,490 --> 00:11:53,780
get the most out of writers,

320
00:11:53,780 --> 00:11:55,700
about how hard writing is.

321
00:11:55,700 --> 00:11:57,080
And I mean it in this way.

322
00:11:57,080 --> 00:11:59,040
Speaking comes so naturally to us,

323
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:00,370
but then writing is something that

324
00:12:00,370 --> 00:12:01,700
we sort of have to learn.

325
00:12:01,700 --> 00:12:03,410
You watch a kid, and you talk

326
00:12:03,410 --> 00:12:05,120
to a parent who has a 20-month-old.

327
00:12:05,120 --> 00:12:06,700
They're like, yeah, they're speaking now.

328
00:12:06,700 --> 00:12:08,540
They're like, oh, you wouldn't believe it.

329
00:12:08,540 --> 00:12:09,700
They're talking so much.

330
00:12:09,700 --> 00:12:10,960
They're crawling around.

331
00:12:10,960 --> 00:12:12,580
And it's always like, yeah, it's like that.

332
00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:15,540
And then you talk to them who have parents

333
00:12:15,540 --> 00:12:16,820
who have eight-year-old kids.

334
00:12:16,820 --> 00:12:17,700
How's the writing going?

335
00:12:17,700 --> 00:12:19,880
It's like, well, it's going a little slow.

336
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:21,200
There's not that same excitement.

337
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:22,820
Why is it that writing

338
00:12:22,820 --> 00:12:24,440
is so unnatural in a way

339
00:12:24,440 --> 00:12:26,080
that speaking is so natural?

340
00:12:26,080 --> 00:12:27,660
What is going on there scientifically

341
00:12:27,660 --> 00:12:29,200
and then practically?

342
00:12:29,200 --> 00:12:30,200
A number of things.

343
00:12:30,200 --> 00:12:36,160
One of them is that in conversation,

344
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:38,160
you never have two people

345
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:40,160
that are parachuted on a stage

346
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:43,280
and immediately have to begin a conversation.

347
00:12:43,280 --> 00:12:46,200
They have some common ground to begin with.

348
00:12:46,200 --> 00:12:48,040
They know why they're there.

349
00:12:48,040 --> 00:12:50,120
They're talking about something that is in the air

350
00:12:50,120 --> 00:12:51,200
that they're both familiar with.

351
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:52,580
It was the reason for them having

352
00:12:52,580 --> 00:12:55,820
the conversation in the first place.

353
00:12:55,820 --> 00:12:59,440
They can get away with using terms that in context

354
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:02,000
are perfectly clear, like this and that and the thing

355
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,200
and what I was talking about and she.

356
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:07,680
Whereas if you are not

357
00:13:07,680 --> 00:13:10,160
privy to that little social circle,

358
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:12,900
you may not know who they're referring to.

359
00:13:12,900 --> 00:13:16,660
In writing, you're wrenched from the context.

360
00:13:16,660 --> 00:13:18,900
Someone's picking a book up off the shelf,

361
00:13:18,900 --> 00:13:21,000
and they've never met you.

362
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:23,580
And they may be living in a different country.

363
00:13:23,580 --> 00:13:25,820
You might be dead.

364
00:13:25,820 --> 00:13:28,700
They've got to pick up all of this detail

365
00:13:28,700 --> 00:13:34,580
from what's there on the page, not the common ground

366
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:37,700
that the two people bring to the conversation.

367
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:40,080
Also, when you're speaking, you know

368
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:42,580
that you're speaking to someone.

369
00:13:42,580 --> 00:13:44,240
You know their idiosyncrasies.

370
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:46,070
You're a little bit better

371
00:13:46,070 --> 00:13:47,900
at avoiding the curse of knowledge,

372
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:51,200
partly because you get feedback, like the furrowed brow.

373
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:52,820
The quizzical expression.

374
00:13:52,820 --> 00:13:54,200
The what?

375
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:55,700
The request for clarification.

376
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:58,200
I'm sorry, I just don't know what you're talking about

377
00:13:58,200 --> 00:13:59,360
in real time.

378
00:13:59,360 --> 00:14:02,440
And the body language, the engagement.

379
00:14:02,440 --> 00:14:07,580
Even in a live audience, any speaker knows when people

380
00:14:07,580 --> 00:14:11,040
are starting to fidget and drift off,

381
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:13,820
as opposed to continuing to be riveted.

382
00:14:13,820 --> 00:14:17,280
None of that is available in writing.

383
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:18,320
Absolutely.

384
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:19,200
How about this one?

385
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:25,960
I think generalizations without examples and examples

386
00:14:25,960 --> 00:14:29,280
without generalizations are both useless.

387
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:30,080
Yes.

388
00:14:30,080 --> 00:14:32,240
Well, and useless might be a bit strong.

389
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:35,960
But generalizations without examples, I find,

390
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:37,900
even in my field, 9 out of 10 times,

391
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:39,740
I just don't know what they're talking about.

392
00:14:39,740 --> 00:14:43,820
It's like, what do you, like, give me an example.

393
00:14:43,820 --> 00:14:49,360
It's too, because a generalization erases detail.

394
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:50,360
It sweeps over.

395
00:14:50,360 --> 00:14:50,860
Mm-hmm.

396
00:14:50,860 --> 00:14:52,780
It sweeps over particulars.

397
00:14:52,780 --> 00:14:56,240
You often just can't really know what it's referring to.

398
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:58,980
And the abstract words in a language

399
00:14:58,980 --> 00:15:01,100
just aren't precise enough.

400
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:04,160
They often have, we have probably 100 concepts

401
00:15:04,160 --> 00:15:06,520
for every word in the English language.

402
00:15:06,520 --> 00:15:10,860
And so a particular word, especially if it's abstract,

403
00:15:10,860 --> 00:15:13,620
won't call to mind a particular referent.

404
00:15:13,620 --> 00:15:20,620
The example pins it down to what's the general ballpark,

405
00:15:20,860 --> 00:15:23,200
what's the ballpark that you're talking about,

406
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:27,360
what is the generalization or generalization about.

407
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:30,160
Now, examples without generalizations, it's,

408
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:31,620
well, why don't you just tell me that?

409
00:15:31,620 --> 00:15:34,020
Like, you know, and your point is?

410
00:15:34,020 --> 00:15:34,520
Yeah.

411
00:15:34,520 --> 00:15:36,860
And apropos of what?

412
00:15:36,860 --> 00:15:38,520
One of my favorite ways to think about this

413
00:15:38,520 --> 00:15:40,740
is that there's always a trade-off between context

414
00:15:40,740 --> 00:15:41,860
and compression.

415
00:15:41,860 --> 00:15:45,880
So if I could wax poetic for

416
00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:49,900
the next five hours about my train

417
00:15:49,900 --> 00:15:50,780
ride to Boston today. But that's fine.

418
00:15:50,780 --> 00:15:52,240
That's way too long.

419
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:53,360
Or I could tell you, yeah,

420
00:15:53,360 --> 00:15:54,480
it was an easy train ride.

421
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:56,660
But then you didn't actually get anything from that,

422
00:15:56,660 --> 00:15:58,240
because there's no context there.

423
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:01,579
And that maybe we're always kind of balancing the examples,

424
00:16:01,579 --> 00:16:03,079
kind of give us that context.

425
00:16:03,079 --> 00:16:05,120
And then the generalizations are the compression.

426
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:07,020
And it's actually in, like, the swing and the dance

427
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:08,400
of the two of those

428
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:09,780
that good writing and good communication

429
00:16:09,780 --> 00:16:10,699
happens.

430
00:16:10,699 --> 00:16:11,280
Yeah.

431
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:13,150
So for example, if I were

432
00:16:13,150 --> 00:16:15,020
to say that familiar words don't

433
00:16:15,020 --> 00:16:19,819
have to refer to the literal meaning of their parts,

434
00:16:19,819 --> 00:16:20,740
you can understand that.

435
00:16:20,740 --> 00:16:23,820
And you kind of say, yeah, yeah, OK.

436
00:16:23,820 --> 00:16:27,860
And then I say, well, for example, a bathroom

437
00:16:27,860 --> 00:16:29,900
isn't necessarily a room with a bath.

438
00:16:29,900 --> 00:16:31,740
And going to the bathroom doesn't necessarily

439
00:16:31,740 --> 00:16:34,120
mean going to a room that has a bathroom.

440
00:16:34,120 --> 00:16:37,120
Breakfast isn't necessarily breaking a fast.

441
00:16:37,120 --> 00:16:40,160
And Christmas doesn't necessarily

442
00:16:40,160 --> 00:16:42,820
refer to Christ's Mass.

443
00:16:42,820 --> 00:16:45,240
Now, having said that, I think you now

444
00:16:45,240 --> 00:16:47,440
understand what I mean when I say

445
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:50,120
that the meaning of a familiar phrase

446
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:53,820
doesn't necessarily correspond to the meanings of its parts.

447
00:16:53,820 --> 00:16:56,580
Hopefully, that now makes some sense to you,

448
00:16:56,580 --> 00:16:58,360
now that I've given you the examples.

449
00:16:58,360 --> 00:17:00,880
Without the examples, you could nod and say,

450
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:01,960
oh, that sounds plausible.

451
00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:03,580
But you really wouldn't have understood it.

452
00:17:03,580 --> 00:17:06,500
Let's roll with that.

453
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:10,240
If adults commit adultery, do infants commit infantry?

454
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:12,619
If olive oil is made from olives,

455
00:17:12,619 --> 00:17:15,319
what do they make baby oil from?

456
00:17:15,319 --> 00:17:17,449
If a vegetarian eats vegetables,

457
00:17:17,449 --> 00:17:19,579
what does a humanitarian consume?

458
00:17:19,579 --> 00:17:22,000
A writer is someone who writes, and a stinger

459
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:23,419
is something that stings.

460
00:17:23,419 --> 00:17:25,399
But fingers don't fing, grocers

461
00:17:25,399 --> 00:17:27,379
don't gross, hammers don't ham,

462
00:17:27,379 --> 00:17:30,379
humdingers don't humding, ushers don't ush,

463
00:17:30,379 --> 00:17:33,199
and haberdashers don't haberdash.

464
00:17:33,199 --> 00:17:34,080
What's going on there?

465
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:35,159
Why did you pull this out?

466
00:17:35,159 --> 00:17:37,120
So this is a quote from Richard Lederer.

467
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:39,659
I deserve no credit for that criticism.

468
00:17:39,659 --> 00:17:40,280
I love this.

469
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:41,040
I think it's so good.

470
00:17:41,040 --> 00:17:43,340
Lederer has written a number of delightful books,

471
00:17:43,340 --> 00:17:46,280
like Crazy English and Anguished English.

472
00:17:46,280 --> 00:17:49,540
He has a marvelous ear and a fabulous,

473
00:17:49,540 --> 00:17:53,960
marvelous collector of quirks and errors and oddities

474
00:17:53,960 --> 00:17:57,500
and blunders in the English language.

475
00:17:57,500 --> 00:18:00,700
So that whimsical list is quite profound

476
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:04,740
because it indicates a lot of truths about language,

477
00:18:04,740 --> 00:18:10,420
such as that over time, compositions of words,

478
00:18:10,420 --> 00:18:13,420
compounds or words with a prefix and suffix,

479
00:18:13,420 --> 00:18:18,420
can sometimes drift away from the original meaning.

480
00:18:18,420 --> 00:18:19,500
The case of adultery,

481
00:18:19,540 --> 00:18:24,159
for example, it is related to adulterate.

482
00:18:24,159 --> 00:18:27,349
Namely, to commit adultery is

483
00:18:27,349 --> 00:18:30,540
to introduce a foreign substance,

484
00:18:30,540 --> 00:18:37,540
namely semen into a woman where it doesn't belong.

485
00:18:37,540 --> 00:18:42,540
But that whole connection has been completely lost.

486
00:18:42,540 --> 00:18:45,540
Also, in many kinds of compounds,

487
00:18:45,540 --> 00:18:49,540
there are a number of different semantic relationships.

488
00:18:49,540 --> 00:18:52,040
So for example, olive oil

489
00:18:52,040 --> 00:18:54,540
is oil made out of olives.

490
00:18:54,540 --> 00:18:58,540
Baby oil is oil for babies.

491
00:18:58,540 --> 00:19:02,540
So there isn't a logical, a single logical relationship

492
00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:05,540
between the different parts of the compound.

493
00:19:05,540 --> 00:19:08,540
Language is so ancient.

494
00:19:08,540 --> 00:19:13,540
English itself is, depending on how you count,

495
00:19:13,540 --> 00:19:17,540
maybe, you know, 1,500 years old or more.

496
00:19:17,540 --> 00:19:18,540
But it came from the early Middle Ages.

497
00:19:18,540 --> 00:19:19,540
So it's a very, very ancient language.

498
00:19:19,540 --> 00:19:22,540
It came from Germanic, which came from Indo-European,

499
00:19:22,540 --> 00:19:24,540
and who knows where that came from,

500
00:19:24,540 --> 00:19:32,540
that a lot of words can completely obscure their origin.

501
00:19:32,540 --> 00:19:33,540
You have to go to

502
00:19:33,540 --> 00:19:34,540
a dictionary to find them out,

503
00:19:34,540 --> 00:19:36,040
but they're kind of like

504
00:19:36,040 --> 00:19:37,540
fossils of processes in the language

505
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:39,540
that are long dead.

506
00:19:39,540 --> 00:19:42,540
You know, how the ER got into finger,

507
00:19:42,540 --> 00:19:44,040
I would have to look

508
00:19:44,040 --> 00:19:45,540
up the etymology to tell you,

509
00:19:45,540 --> 00:19:48,540
but it's not the same as, say, sing-singer,

510
00:19:48,540 --> 00:19:51,040
which comes from the rule that

511
00:19:51,040 --> 00:19:53,540
ER turns a verb into a noun

512
00:19:53,540 --> 00:19:55,040
for the kind of person

513
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:56,540
that typically engages in that activity.

514
00:19:56,540 --> 00:19:58,540
That's a rule that's still alive

515
00:19:58,540 --> 00:20:00,540
and well in the English language,

516
00:20:00,540 --> 00:20:03,540
whereas we have the fossil record of

517
00:20:03,540 --> 00:20:06,540
lots of rules that died long ago.

518
00:20:06,540 --> 00:20:08,540
When you're writing, like, if

519
00:20:08,540 --> 00:20:10,540
I were to basically map out

520
00:20:10,540 --> 00:20:12,540
almost like a gradient of our conversation,

521
00:20:12,540 --> 00:20:15,540
I'd say like, curse of knowledge, I would almost,

522
00:20:15,540 --> 00:20:16,540
I'm being very haphazard here, I'd say

523
00:20:16,540 --> 00:20:17,540
like equal parts left brain, right brain,

524
00:20:17,540 --> 00:20:20,040
and then we sort of moved into kind

525
00:20:20,040 --> 00:20:22,540
of a left brain side of the conversation.

526
00:20:22,540 --> 00:20:24,540
And then I think of what you've said

527
00:20:24,540 --> 00:20:26,540
about language should be a source of pleasure.

528
00:20:26,540 --> 00:20:28,540
Like, I think of that in beauty, right?

529
00:20:28,540 --> 00:20:30,540
You talk a lot about beauty also in your photography.

530
00:20:30,540 --> 00:20:32,540
You're very interested in that.

531
00:20:32,540 --> 00:20:34,540
That to me is more right brain.

532
00:20:34,540 --> 00:20:36,540
So like when you're writing,

533
00:20:36,540 --> 00:20:38,540
when you're actually sitting down at the keyboard,

534
00:20:38,540 --> 00:20:40,540
what are some of the more right brained,

535
00:20:40,540 --> 00:20:43,540
maybe less verbal concepts that drive your writing?

536
00:20:43,540 --> 00:20:45,540
Well, certainly visual imagery.

537
00:20:45,540 --> 00:20:47,540
That is, can I write?

538
00:20:47,540 --> 00:20:48,540
Can I form an image?

539
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:50,540
Can I read or form an image?

540
00:20:50,540 --> 00:20:52,540
Euphony, that is sound.

541
00:20:52,540 --> 00:20:57,540
That is, is there some poetry in the prose?

542
00:20:57,540 --> 00:21:01,540
And I read aloud or at least mumble,

543
00:21:01,540 --> 00:21:04,540
or at least mumble to myself my prose,

544
00:21:04,540 --> 00:21:06,540
something that's, again, a highly recommended writing tip.

545
00:21:06,540 --> 00:21:07,540
I didn't invent it.

546
00:21:07,540 --> 00:21:10,540
Often when you read a draft of your own prose,

547
00:21:10,540 --> 00:21:13,540
and if you can't articulate it smoothly,

548
00:21:13,540 --> 00:21:17,540
that probably your reader won't be,

549
00:21:17,540 --> 00:21:20,540
mentally sounding it out smoothly either.

550
00:21:20,540 --> 00:21:26,540
The aesthetics sometimes come from even things like

551
00:21:26,540 --> 00:21:29,540
paying attention to the metrical structure of language.

552
00:21:29,540 --> 00:21:30,540
That is the rhythm.

553
00:21:30,540 --> 00:21:31,540
Oh, tell me about that.

554
00:21:31,540 --> 00:21:34,540
There is a regular rhythm to language

555
00:21:34,540 --> 00:21:37,540
in that it's not perfectly, you know,

556
00:21:37,540 --> 00:21:39,540
tick-tock, tick-tock like a metronome,

557
00:21:39,540 --> 00:21:41,540
but there are beats.

558
00:21:41,540 --> 00:21:43,540
And if you disrupt it too much,

559
00:21:43,540 --> 00:21:46,540
then it does interfere with speech,

560
00:21:46,540 --> 00:21:48,540
but it also interferes with reading,

561
00:21:48,540 --> 00:21:50,540
even though it's just characters on a page.

562
00:21:50,540 --> 00:21:55,540
Even the aesthetics of sibilant sounds,

563
00:21:55,540 --> 00:22:00,540
generally too many chs and ss

564
00:22:00,540 --> 00:22:03,540
make prose a little unpleasant,

565
00:22:03,540 --> 00:22:07,540
and I will often pick a synonym

566
00:22:07,540 --> 00:22:09,540
that avoids the sibilant,

567
00:22:09,540 --> 00:22:12,540
at least too many sibilants in a row.

568
00:22:12,540 --> 00:22:15,540
I'll often go with alliteration,

569
00:22:15,540 --> 00:22:17,540
just because it, again,

570
00:22:17,540 --> 00:22:19,540
there's a little spark of pleasure.

571
00:22:19,540 --> 00:22:20,540
Sense of style.

572
00:22:20,540 --> 00:22:22,540
Sense of style, yep.

573
00:22:22,540 --> 00:22:23,540
Even when it is,

574
00:22:23,540 --> 00:22:26,540
you don't want to make it too conspicuous,

575
00:22:26,540 --> 00:22:28,540
otherwise it starts to feel forced.

576
00:22:28,540 --> 00:22:31,540
But often a bit of alliteration

577
00:22:31,540 --> 00:22:35,540
can just make the sentence roll past more easily.

578
00:22:35,540 --> 00:22:37,540
Why do you think that you,

579
00:22:37,540 --> 00:22:38,540
as much as anybody,

580
00:22:38,540 --> 00:22:39,540
have been the person to kind of

581
00:22:39,540 --> 00:22:41,540
stomp your feet, kick and scream

582
00:22:41,540 --> 00:22:44,540
about the, how bad academic writing

583
00:22:44,540 --> 00:22:45,540
has been over the years?

584
00:22:45,540 --> 00:22:47,540
Like when I think of who has been like,

585
00:22:47,540 --> 00:22:49,540
guys, what are we doing?

586
00:22:49,540 --> 00:22:51,540
You've sort of been one of the front runners.

587
00:22:51,540 --> 00:22:52,540
What about it?

588
00:22:52,540 --> 00:22:53,540
I mean, besides the obvious,

589
00:22:53,540 --> 00:22:56,540
what about it has just bothered you so vividly?

590
00:22:56,540 --> 00:23:00,540
Yeah, part of it is just the sheer waste.

591
00:23:00,540 --> 00:23:04,540
That is, there's an awful lot of really brilliant work,

592
00:23:04,540 --> 00:23:07,540
a lot of really smart people in academia,

593
00:23:07,540 --> 00:23:09,540
and why are they doing it?

594
00:23:09,540 --> 00:23:13,540
Just to entertain each other in a closed little circle?

595
00:23:13,540 --> 00:23:14,540
I mean, taxpayers pay for it.

596
00:23:14,540 --> 00:23:16,540
It should be accessible.

597
00:23:16,540 --> 00:23:17,540
They should give it away.

598
00:23:17,540 --> 00:23:20,540
They should state it in a form that it's accessible.

599
00:23:20,540 --> 00:23:24,540
There's also, when even within the profession,

600
00:23:24,540 --> 00:23:27,540
there's just an enormous amount of wasted effort

601
00:23:27,540 --> 00:23:30,540
and potential for misunderstanding in bad prose.

602
00:23:30,540 --> 00:23:31,540
It's like if I'm reading,

603
00:23:31,540 --> 00:23:33,540
if I have to read something,

604
00:23:33,540 --> 00:23:34,540
it's a student paper,

605
00:23:34,540 --> 00:23:36,540
or it's a paper in my own field,

606
00:23:36,540 --> 00:23:38,540
or I'm reviewing a grant proposal,

607
00:23:38,540 --> 00:23:39,540
or peer reviewing a manuscript,

608
00:23:39,540 --> 00:23:41,540
or evaluating for someone for a grant,

609
00:23:41,540 --> 00:23:43,540
it's like, I'm reading a paper

610
00:23:43,540 --> 00:23:45,540
and I'm evaluating for someone for tenure.

611
00:23:45,540 --> 00:23:47,540
If I have to read the same paragraph

612
00:23:47,540 --> 00:23:48,540
like five or six times

613
00:23:48,540 --> 00:23:50,540
in order to know what they're talking about,

614
00:23:50,540 --> 00:23:52,540
for one thing, I might get it wrong,

615
00:23:52,540 --> 00:23:53,540
then what's the point?

616
00:23:53,540 --> 00:23:54,540
In other words,

617
00:23:54,540 --> 00:23:55,540
why should I have to

618
00:23:55,540 --> 00:23:56,540
read it five or six times?

619
00:23:56,540 --> 00:23:58,540
I'd rather be doing something else.

620
00:23:58,540 --> 00:23:59,540
Come on.

621
00:23:59,540 --> 00:24:00,540
Yeah.

622
00:24:00,540 --> 00:24:03,540
So there's waste, there's confusion.

623
00:24:03,540 --> 00:24:07,540
There's also foregone opportunity for pleasure and beauty.

624
00:24:07,540 --> 00:24:11,540
It's enjoyable to read something that's well-crafted

625
00:24:11,540 --> 00:24:13,540
and it's annoying to read something

626
00:24:13,540 --> 00:24:18,540
that's stilted and turgid and bloated.

627
00:24:18,540 --> 00:24:20,540
There are many reasons,

628
00:24:20,540 --> 00:24:23,540
and it does get under my skin

629
00:24:23,540 --> 00:24:26,540
when academics devote so much brain power

630
00:24:26,540 --> 00:24:28,540
into the scholarship

631
00:24:28,540 --> 00:24:32,540
and then just blow off the essential task

632
00:24:32,540 --> 00:24:34,540
of letting the world know what you've done.

633
00:24:34,540 --> 00:24:37,540
Yeah, it's a true virus in the modern world.

634
00:24:37,540 --> 00:24:38,540
Yeah.

635
00:24:38,540 --> 00:24:40,540
I take a particular interest in it,

636
00:24:40,540 --> 00:24:43,540
not just because I do try very hard,

637
00:24:43,540 --> 00:24:46,540
but even in my academic writing, to be clear.

638
00:24:46,540 --> 00:24:50,540
But since I'm someone who actually

639
00:24:50,540 --> 00:24:52,540
not just studies stuff as an academic,

640
00:24:52,540 --> 00:24:54,540
but studies language,

641
00:24:54,540 --> 00:24:55,540
Yeah.

642
00:24:55,540 --> 00:24:57,540
I get particularly annoyed at people

643
00:24:57,540 --> 00:24:59,540
in the field of linguistics and psycholinguistics

644
00:24:59,540 --> 00:25:01,540
because so many of them are such bad writers.

645
00:25:01,540 --> 00:25:03,540
It's like, you study language.

646
00:25:03,540 --> 00:25:05,540
If what you're studying is

647
00:25:05,540 --> 00:25:07,540
what makes a sentence difficult to understand,

648
00:25:07,540 --> 00:25:10,540
why don't you pay attention to your own research

649
00:25:10,540 --> 00:25:12,540
and make your sentences less hard to understand?

650
00:25:13,540 --> 00:25:14,540
Yeah.

651
00:25:14,540 --> 00:25:16,540
What is it that you love so much

652
00:25:16,540 --> 00:25:17,540
about children's explanations?

653
00:25:17,540 --> 00:25:19,540
Things like clouds are water vapor,

654
00:25:19,540 --> 00:25:21,540
smoke is fire vapor.

655
00:25:21,540 --> 00:25:22,540
Oh, yes.

656
00:25:22,540 --> 00:25:24,540
You do see things...

657
00:25:24,540 --> 00:25:27,540
Yeah, that was from my grandson.

658
00:25:27,540 --> 00:25:31,540
I mean, it's poetry.

659
00:25:31,540 --> 00:25:33,540
It's a new juxtaposition.

660
00:25:33,540 --> 00:25:35,540
There's such a freshness to children's explanations.

661
00:25:35,540 --> 00:25:38,540
Yeah, yeah, because they aren't writing in cliches,

662
00:25:38,540 --> 00:25:42,540
and partly because they haven't accumulated

663
00:25:42,540 --> 00:25:43,540
the knowledge.

664
00:25:43,540 --> 00:25:45,540
They have this massive abstractions,

665
00:25:45,540 --> 00:25:48,540
like the writers of a few centuries ago

666
00:25:48,540 --> 00:25:52,540
who had to grasp for some common image.

667
00:25:52,540 --> 00:25:56,540
Children, without the decades

668
00:25:56,540 --> 00:26:01,540
of acquired jargon from academia,

669
00:26:01,540 --> 00:26:03,540
have to appeal to something that they can see

670
00:26:03,540 --> 00:26:05,540
and that other people can see.

671
00:26:05,540 --> 00:26:06,540
Yeah.

672
00:26:06,540 --> 00:26:09,540
There's a guy who I follow on Twitter

673
00:26:09,540 --> 00:26:11,540
who I've really come to like,

674
00:26:11,540 --> 00:26:12,540
and in his bio it says,

675
00:26:12,540 --> 00:26:13,540
listen to children.

676
00:26:13,540 --> 00:26:15,540
They haven't forgotten how to see.

677
00:26:15,540 --> 00:26:18,540
And I know a YouTuber,

678
00:26:18,540 --> 00:26:19,540
and one of the ways that

679
00:26:19,540 --> 00:26:20,540
she comes up with her ideas

680
00:26:20,540 --> 00:26:23,540
is she has conversations with her friends' kids

681
00:26:23,540 --> 00:26:25,540
who just ask these crazy questions,

682
00:26:25,540 --> 00:26:27,540
like how deep is the ocean?

683
00:26:27,540 --> 00:26:30,540
How high would a tower need to be

684
00:26:30,540 --> 00:26:31,540
in order for us to get to heaven?

685
00:26:31,540 --> 00:26:33,540
And then, like, I have another friend

686
00:26:33,540 --> 00:26:34,540
who, when he was a kid,

687
00:26:34,540 --> 00:26:36,540
he thought that clouds were produced

688
00:26:36,540 --> 00:26:37,540
by those little smoke things,

689
00:26:37,540 --> 00:26:39,540
sort of like chimneys,

690
00:26:39,540 --> 00:26:41,540
so he thought that those were like cloud creators.

691
00:26:41,540 --> 00:26:42,540
And there's such a freshness

692
00:26:42,540 --> 00:26:44,540
to just how children see

693
00:26:44,540 --> 00:26:45,540
and how they talk about things.

694
00:26:45,540 --> 00:26:46,540
And like you said,

695
00:26:46,540 --> 00:26:50,540
they can't possibly think inside the box

696
00:26:50,540 --> 00:26:52,540
because they don't even know the box exists.

697
00:26:52,540 --> 00:26:53,540
Yes, right.

698
00:26:53,540 --> 00:26:55,540
And there were,

699
00:26:55,540 --> 00:26:57,540
there have been in history

700
00:26:57,540 --> 00:26:59,540
various features to remind people

701
00:26:59,540 --> 00:27:03,540
of the originality and the freshness of kids.

702
00:27:03,540 --> 00:27:06,540
There was a regular feature from my childhood

703
00:27:06,540 --> 00:27:08,540
called Kids See the Darnedest Things

704
00:27:08,540 --> 00:27:11,540
from a television host named Ark Lipschitz,

705
00:27:11,540 --> 00:27:12,540
Art Linkletter.

706
00:27:12,540 --> 00:27:13,540
Yeah, I've heard of him.

707
00:27:13,540 --> 00:27:14,540
Oh, you have?

708
00:27:14,540 --> 00:27:15,540
Okay.

709
00:27:15,540 --> 00:27:16,540
Yeah.

710
00:27:16,540 --> 00:27:17,540
In fact, what has survived him

711
00:27:17,540 --> 00:27:21,540
and the feature of the program

712
00:27:21,540 --> 00:27:23,540
is the Say the Darnedest Things

713
00:27:23,540 --> 00:27:26,540
or even the verb the darnedest nouns,

714
00:27:26,540 --> 00:27:30,540
which you'll often see adopted in headlines

715
00:27:30,540 --> 00:27:33,540
and it's become itself a kind of formula,

716
00:27:33,540 --> 00:27:34,540
but he originated it.

717
00:27:34,540 --> 00:27:35,540
Johnny Carson,

718
00:27:35,540 --> 00:27:38,540
the longtime host of The Tonight Show,

719
00:27:38,540 --> 00:27:40,540
would sometimes have features

720
00:27:40,540 --> 00:27:44,540
where he'd read answers,

721
00:27:44,540 --> 00:27:48,540
kids' answers to questions to much hilarity.

722
00:27:48,540 --> 00:27:51,540
How does humor factor into all this?

723
00:27:51,540 --> 00:27:52,540
Into writing?

724
00:27:52,540 --> 00:27:53,540
Yeah, exactly.

725
00:27:53,540 --> 00:27:57,540
Like, and even maybe even the science of humor,

726
00:27:57,540 --> 00:27:59,540
like words that are funnier,

727
00:27:59,540 --> 00:28:00,540
because you have that great

728
00:28:00,540 --> 00:28:01,540
talk from like 10 years ago

729
00:28:01,540 --> 00:28:02,540
where you, I'm going to recommend it.

730
00:28:02,540 --> 00:28:03,540
Yeah, well, there's,

731
00:28:03,540 --> 00:28:05,540
I guess like everything, there's an art to it

732
00:28:05,540 --> 00:28:07,540
because you don't want to,

733
00:28:07,540 --> 00:28:09,540
when it's strained, you get groans

734
00:28:09,540 --> 00:28:10,540
instead of laughter.

735
00:28:10,540 --> 00:28:11,540
Yeah.

736
00:28:11,540 --> 00:28:13,540
So it has to be appropriate.

737
00:28:13,540 --> 00:28:15,540
It can't just be something

738
00:28:15,540 --> 00:28:17,540
that has been repeated so often

739
00:28:17,540 --> 00:28:20,540
that people have heard it 37 times before.

740
00:28:20,540 --> 00:28:21,540
Right.

741
00:28:21,540 --> 00:28:24,540
And humor specifically depends on freshness.

742
00:28:24,540 --> 00:28:25,540
Yeah, it does.

743
00:28:25,540 --> 00:28:26,540
And it also depends,

744
00:28:26,540 --> 00:28:32,540
humor does have much in common with good writing.

745
00:28:32,540 --> 00:28:34,540
There's a line from Hamlet,

746
00:28:34,540 --> 00:28:38,540
which brevity is the soul of wit.

747
00:28:38,540 --> 00:28:39,540
Yeah.

748
00:28:39,540 --> 00:28:42,540
Now, it's a great saying for a number of reasons.

749
00:28:42,540 --> 00:28:45,540
One of them is even the term soul,

750
00:28:45,540 --> 00:28:48,540
as opposed to is essential for wit,

751
00:28:48,540 --> 00:28:50,540
or is important for wit,

752
00:28:50,540 --> 00:28:52,540
or even the essence of wit.

753
00:28:52,540 --> 00:28:54,540
Those are ways that we would say it now,

754
00:28:54,540 --> 00:28:55,540
but the soul of wit,

755
00:28:55,540 --> 00:28:57,540
you have to think for a minute,

756
00:28:57,540 --> 00:29:00,540
soul being the deepest, deepest essence,

757
00:29:00,540 --> 00:29:02,540
but it's a monosyllable,

758
00:29:02,540 --> 00:29:06,540
and it has so much resonance.

759
00:29:06,540 --> 00:29:07,540
Yeah.

760
00:29:07,540 --> 00:29:09,040
I guess that Shakespeare really

761
00:29:09,040 --> 00:29:10,540
was a good writer, wasn't he?

762
00:29:10,540 --> 00:29:11,540
Yeah, I've heard of that guy.

763
00:29:11,540 --> 00:29:12,540
Yeah.

764
00:29:12,540 --> 00:29:13,540
But it's also,

765
00:29:13,540 --> 00:29:16,540
it is true and it's a good example of itself

766
00:29:16,540 --> 00:29:18,540
because it is so brief.

767
00:29:18,540 --> 00:29:21,540
It was reiterated centuries later

768
00:29:21,540 --> 00:29:22,540
in the famous style manual,

769
00:29:22,540 --> 00:29:26,540
The Elements of Style by Strunk and White,

770
00:29:26,540 --> 00:29:29,540
where Professor Strunk,

771
00:29:29,540 --> 00:29:33,540
in lectures at Cornell that E.B. White recalled

772
00:29:33,540 --> 00:29:34,540
and then turned to,

773
00:29:34,540 --> 00:29:35,540
he said,

774
00:29:35,540 --> 00:29:39,540
that the first rule of writing is omit needless words.

775
00:29:39,540 --> 00:29:42,540
Now, again, it's a beautiful example of itself

776
00:29:42,540 --> 00:29:44,540
because there are no needless words in it.

777
00:29:44,540 --> 00:29:46,540
And he said that sometimes Professor Strunk

778
00:29:46,540 --> 00:29:50,540
would be so adamant and so insistent

779
00:29:50,540 --> 00:29:52,540
that this was almost the only thing

780
00:29:52,540 --> 00:29:53,540
you needed to know about writing

781
00:29:53,540 --> 00:29:54,540
that he would just not know

782
00:29:54,540 --> 00:29:55,540
what to do except repeat it.

783
00:29:55,540 --> 00:29:56,540
And he'd say,

784
00:29:56,540 --> 00:29:58,540
omit needless words, omit needless words,

785
00:29:58,540 --> 00:29:59,540
omit needless words.

786
00:29:59,540 --> 00:30:00,540
And he would say,

787
00:30:00,540 --> 00:30:01,540
I'm not going to repeat it.

788
00:30:01,540 --> 00:30:02,540
I'm going to omit it.

789
00:30:02,540 --> 00:30:03,540
And he would say,

790
00:30:03,540 --> 00:30:04,540
I'm going to omit it.

791
00:30:04,540 --> 00:30:05,540
I'm going to omit it.

792
00:30:05,540 --> 00:30:06,540
I'm going to omit it.

793
00:30:06,540 --> 00:30:07,540
And I would say,

794
00:30:07,540 --> 00:30:08,540
I'm going to omit it.

795
00:30:08,540 --> 00:30:09,540
I'm going to omit needless words.

796
00:30:09,540 --> 00:30:10,540
And as with brevity is the soul of wit,

797
00:30:10,540 --> 00:30:12,540
aside from being an example of itself,

798
00:30:12,540 --> 00:30:15,540
it is so true that sometimes

799
00:30:15,540 --> 00:30:18,540
when I have to compress an article

800
00:30:18,540 --> 00:30:20,540
to fit into some fixed space,

801
00:30:20,540 --> 00:30:22,540
especially if I'm writing for a newspaper or a magazine,

802
00:30:22,540 --> 00:30:24,540
it's unlike academia

803
00:30:24,540 --> 00:30:27,540
where you can blather on

804
00:30:27,540 --> 00:30:29,540
with no one telling you to shut up.

805
00:30:29,540 --> 00:30:31,540
When you have to write for a paper,

806
00:30:31,540 --> 00:30:32,540
they'll say,

807
00:30:32,540 --> 00:30:33,540
sorry, 800 words.

808
00:30:33,540 --> 00:30:34,540
And if it's not,

809
00:30:34,540 --> 00:30:38,000
know, 803 words, the editor will chop off three words,

810
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:39,500
and you don't get to say what they are.

811
00:30:39,500 --> 00:30:41,260
And it can often mangle the meaning.

812
00:30:41,260 --> 00:30:43,360
So you have to actually learn how

813
00:30:43,360 --> 00:30:45,100
to say something in 800 words

814
00:30:45,100 --> 00:30:46,840
or however many words it is.

815
00:30:46,840 --> 00:30:49,749
But what I find is often

816
00:30:49,749 --> 00:30:52,659
just the exercise of squeezing it

817
00:30:52,659 --> 00:30:57,580
into that maximum limit just improves the prose

818
00:30:57,580 --> 00:30:58,840
as if by magic.

